Air and Taylor Johnson, as the next James Bond, is priced at 6% on Kalshi. Access depends

00:02:50.309 --> 00:02:55.029
on where you live, because country, province, and state rules are not the same, and they

00:02:55.029 --> 00:03:00.311
keep changing. Copying the last trade instead of using what you know is how an information

00:03:00.311 --> 00:03:04.951
cascade shoves a price off the evidence, and a price that can move is supposed to stop

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:12.631
that. Avery and Zemsky laid out the theory in 1998, and a 2005 American Economic Review

00:03:12.631 --> 00:03:19.471
Internet experiment, put it to a test. More than 6,400 people took part. 267 of them

00:03:19.471 --> 00:03:24.491
consultants at an international firm. Subjects traded in a market where the price was free

00:03:24.491 --> 00:03:29.531
to move, so earlier trades were already in the number. That flexible price stopped hurting.

00:03:30.051 --> 00:03:34.731
People did not just stack onto the previous trade and drop their own signal. Contrarian

00:03:34.731 --> 00:03:40.391
trades still yanked prices away from the evidence. Even after 20 decisions, landing on the fundamental

00:03:40.391 --> 00:03:45.231
value was rare. Those consultants did not act in a meaningfully different way from the

00:03:45.231 --> 00:03:51.091
rest of the group. A 2007 Journal of Finance paper sat Chicago Board of Trade Professionals

00:03:51.091 --> 00:03:56.111
in a controlled setting next to students. Across more than 1,500 individual decisions,

00:03:56.591 --> 00:04:00.951
the professionals were better at telling a strong public signal from a week one. Their

00:04:00.951 --> 00:04:05.331
choices also did not shift with the domain of earnings, and the student group did not

00:04:05.331 --> 00:04:10.871
show that same pattern. The authors read the gap as a point about market efficiency. Who is

00:04:10.871 --> 00:04:16.831
trading changes how public signals get used. Prosper.com, then the biggest micro-loan market in the

00:04:16.831 --> 00:04:22.651
country, is where a 2012 management science paper went looking for herds. Listings that were

00:04:22.651 --> 00:04:27.011
already well-funded drew still more money after controls for hidden listing differences,

00:04:27.551 --> 00:04:32.371
and for payoff externalities. Lenders were learning from peers, not only mimicking them,

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:36.471
and they used public borrower facts to temper what the crowds seemed to say.

00:04:36.731 --> 00:04:41.471
The paper calls that rational herding, and it calls passive copying irrational herding.

00:04:42.011 --> 00:04:46.751
A bad credit grade made the herd stronger, because funders inferred the borrower had to

00:04:46.751 --> 00:04:49.411
be better than the grade, or the money would not have shown up.

00:04:49.911 --> 00:04:53.871
A friend endorsement made the herd weaker because the crowd could be blamed on a fact

00:04:53.871 --> 00:04:55.231
everyone could already see.

00:04:55.891 --> 00:04:59.911
Rational herding predicted how loans performed better than irrational herding did.

00:04:59.911 --> 00:05:07.331
Crypto got the same question in a 2018 annals of financial economics paper using the CSAD method

00:05:07.331 --> 00:05:09.571
from Chang and co-authors in 2000.

00:05:10.371 --> 00:05:20.391
Daily returns covered six major cryptocurrencies plus the CCI-30 index from 07082015 to 18012018.

00:05:21.151 --> 00:05:26.511
Hurting appeared in rising markets and in falling ones, which the paper treats as over-enthusiasm

00:05:26.511 --> 00:05:27.551
and over-reaction.

00:05:28.231 --> 00:05:30.891
Volatility did not matter in a significant way.

00:05:31.251 --> 00:05:33.511
What mattered was how active the market was.

00:05:33.971 --> 00:05:39.791
A 2013 review in the review of Behavioral Finance grants two decades of real insight and

00:05:39.791 --> 00:05:42.311
still calls the evidence inconclusive in spots.

00:05:42.831 --> 00:05:47.691
Relatively little is known once you leave equity markets, and the review wants methods that

00:05:47.691 --> 00:05:49.451
fix the limits of the ones in use.

00:05:50.011 --> 00:05:55.031
A loan tally, a crypto index, and a trading floor experiment do not automatically carry

00:05:55.031 --> 00:05:56.851
over to an event contract book.

00:05:56.851 --> 00:06:04.731
So a fast move can be people ignoring what they know, or a price that already folded earlier trades in, so the next copy is empty.

00:06:05.271 --> 00:06:11.691
It can also be contrarians pulling the number off the signals, the miss that experiment still found after 20 decisions.

00:06:12.191 --> 00:06:19.051
And a fact sitting in plain sight can explain the pylon with no hidden insight behind it, the way those friend endorsements did.

00:06:19.531 --> 00:06:22.951
That is a way to read a price, it is not a reason to pick a side.

